      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Sunday's loss bled into Monday morning like a bad seam in the clubhouse door - Toronto had one clean win in the series, then watched Boston answer with a 6-1 pummeling that left the Blue Jays at 48-58, 5th in the AL East and 14.5 games back. <strong>That 6-1 thud from last night was the latest reminder this club can still flash - then disappear.</strong> Kevin Gausman's line in what may have been his final start for the Jays was decent enough, but the offense never supplied the Red Sox-style surge it needed, managing just one run after a 6-0 shutout Saturday. The broader picture is blunt: a one-game skid, a minus-5 run differential, and a roster that keeps asking too much of too few bats. <strong>Now comes Washington on Monday night, and Toronto needs a quick response, not another flat footed start.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated July 27, 2026"
      },
